0.3 re-fitted post-fix: b is 4.08x smaller, not gone, and a is unchanged

The ladder's cost model, re-fitted against a binary rebuilt from HEAD, on a controlled
context sweep instead of the battery corpus (which cannot be re-run):

                pre-fix (battery, n 2158)     post-fix (sweep, n 48)
  a             136.44 +/- 0.52 ms            130.98 +/- 2.25 ms
  b              30.051 +/- 0.241 ms/1000       7.362 +/- 0.370 ms/1000
  b x 6592      198.10 ms                      48.53 +/- 2.44 ms
  context        71 - 6592                    249 - 12,410
  tau p50         2.91                          1.68

R^2 0.971. NEITHER STOP CONDITION IS MET: a is 1.59x its 82.18 ms byte floor against a
target of 1.25x, and b x 6592 is 9.7x the 5.0 ms threshold. The loop continues.

This SUPERSEDES 0.2's headline that "the context term is GONE". 0.2 was two single runs at
ctx 480 and 3000, where the residual term is 22 ms of a 153 ms forward -- invisible at the
measured 3.5% run-to-run spread. At ctx 12,410 it is 91 ms, 41% of the forward. New item
0.4 re-profiles at 12k, because 0.2's retirement of items 1.2 and 1.5 rests on i:topk =
0.10 ms and i:score = 0.02 ms measured at ctx <= 3000, and those two marks cannot account
for 91 ms at any context unless they grow.

a must be read as UNCHANGED, not improved. This corpus runs at tau 1.68 against the
battery's 2.91, so fewer positions are verified per forward and a smaller expert union is
read; 130.98 is the optimistic end of the same number, for reasons that have nothing to do
with any kernel. The b comparison is the sound one -- each is a slope fitted within its own
corpus, and the ~4x is far outside either interval.

The slope is not a speculation artifact. Realised verify width correlates +0.35 with
context here and width is what sets bytes per forward, so the raw slope could have been an
acceptance effect in disguise. Fitting both: fwd = 70.74 + 7.029 x (ctx/1000) + 23.04 x
width, R^2 0.985. Width costs a real 23 ms per unit and absorbs 0.33 of the 7.36; the
context term survives at 7.03 +/- 0.28 with width held fixed.

The cheap prefill was checked, not assumed. The sweep descends through token-prefixes of
one document so the engine's longest-common-prefix cache turns 47 of 48 prefills into a
rewind_to. If rewind_to left the compressed or index caches cheaper than a freshly built
context, every number would describe a path the engine does not ship. Four control legs
built their context from a different document: 181.59 vs 179.18 ms/forward at ctx ~6250,
and 150.09 vs 147.66 at ctx ~1660 -- both inside the 3.5% spread.

Instruments:
  tools/decode_fit_probe.py  new. Detached, named in detach_audit.sh, fatal on transport
                             failure, writes no record for a leg that generated nothing.
                             --report re-derives every table above from the records.
  tools/decode_model.py      --dir, plus standard errors on both coefficients. Once b is
                             small R^2 is the wrong statistic: a genuinely flat slope
                             scores R^2 ~ 0 however precisely the zero is known, and the
                             stop condition is a claim about an interval on b.

Run hygiene: server rebuilt from HEAD (all six CPU gates pass), launched detached via
run_server.sh at seqmax 16384 with a memguard armed, mem 120.0/122.8 GiB, and stopped at
the end of the iteration -- nothing is resident. No kernel changed, so no bit-exactness
question arises; tau is reported at every point.

